import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Nav
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Button
import ../../Ui/Feature
import ../../Ui/FeatureGrid
import ../../Ui/PageCards
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/Way

## rocx's overview: what the markup is, why it is shaped the way it is, and its pages, as cards.
meta = {
	title: "rocx: markup in Roc",
	description: "rocx is Roc Solid's markup: Roc in which markup may stand wherever an expression can. rocx build turns each file into plain Roc with the same line numbers, so the Roc compiler checks every value put in a page; the server renders the page, and the browser runs the same Roc to make it live.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "why", label: "Why rocx" },
		{ id: "kinds", label: "Pages, components, endpoints" },
		{ id: "build", label: "A file, and the Roc it becomes" },
		{ id: "pages", label: "rocx's pages" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="rocx · Roc Solid's markup"
				title="Write pages and components in Roc. Render them on the server. Run them in the browser."
				actions={[
					<Button href={Paths.syntax} primary>Learn the syntax</Button>,
					<Button href={Paths.components}>Components</Button>,
				]}
			>
				A <code>.rocx</code> file is a Roc file in which you may write markup wherever an expression can
				go. <code>rocx build</code> turns it into plain Roc with the same line numbers, and the Roc
				compiler checks it like any other Roc. Pages, layouts and components are all written this way.
				The server renders a page to HTML; in the browser, the same Roc, compiled to WebAssembly, takes
				that HTML over and keeps it up to date. What only the server needs to show stays plain HTML,
				inside a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code>.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="why">Why rocx</Heading>
			<FeatureGrid>
				<Feature title="Checked by Roc">
					<p>
						Every <code>{"{hole}"}</code> in markup is a Roc expression, and the compiler checks it: a
						hole shows text, a number or markup, a list hole takes a <code>List(Html)</code>, an
						attribute a <code>Str</code>, and a handler must be a function of the right type. Mistakes
						are reported at your <code>.rocx</code> line, quoting your code.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Safe by construction">
					<p>
						<code>Html</code> is an opaque type, and markup has no way to turn a string into markup.
						Text and attribute values are escaped, URLs that could run script are replaced, and handlers
						are Roc functions, never script in an attribute (<a href={Paths.safety}>safety</a>).
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Line for line">
					<p>
						Line <var>N</var> of the generated Roc is line <var>N</var> of your file, so the compiler's
						reports, <code>dbg</code> and crash messages already point to the right place. There are no
						source maps to break.
					</p>
				</Feature>
				<Feature title="Fine-grained, end to end">
					<p>
						As in SolidJS, a component runs once. The server renders the page whole. In the browser, a
						change runs only the holes that read what changed, and each patches its own node: nothing is
						rendered again to find out what changed.
					</p>
				</Feature>
			</FeatureGrid>

			<Heading id="kinds">Pages, components, endpoints</Heading>
			<Way kind="Pages" href={Paths.routing} more="Routing →" code={[<Code source={Sources.hello.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />]}>
				A page is a file, <code>routes/…/+page.rocx</code>, at the URL its folders spell. It is a
				component: the server renders it for each request, inside the layouts above it, and the browser
				hydrates it. What it wraps in <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> stays the server's HTML.
			</Way>
			<Way kind="Components" href={Paths.components} more="Components →" code={[<Code source={Sources.home_update.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />]}>
				SolidJS 2.0's model: a component runs once and may make signals, values that change. Each hole
				that reads a signal is a small computation that runs again when the signal changes, and patches
				its own node. A page puts a component wherever it wants it, as it would an element.
			</Way>
			<Way kind="Endpoints" href={Paths.calls} more="Talking to the server →" code={[<Code source={Sources.way_endpoint.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />]}>
				A route's <code>+server.roc</code> holds its endpoints: a function per HTTP method at a URL,
				typed at both ends. A component reads a GET as a plain value. The server answers that read while
				it renders the page, and the page carries the answer to the browser. The handler's code runs only
				on the server and is not in the browser's download.
			</Way>

			<Heading id="build">A file, and the Roc it becomes</Heading>
			<p>
				The counter on the <a href={Paths.root}>front page</a> is this file:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.demo_counter.file} source={Sources.demo_counter.rocx} />
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> writes <code>Counter.roc</code> beside it. Its lines 9 to 20 are these,
				the same lines as in the file above, with the markup turned into calls to the platform's
				<code>Html</code> module:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="Counter.roc, as rocx build writes it"
				source={"view! : { start_value : () => I64 } => Html\nview! = |{ start_value: start_value! }| {\n\t(count!, set_count!) = create_signal!(start_value!())\n\tHtml.element(\"div\", [Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"counter rx-49lxy6\"), Html.class_of(\"negative\", || count!() < 0),], [\n\t\tHtml.element(\"button\", [Html.attr_literal(\"type\", \"button\"), Html.on(\"click\", || set_count!(count!() - 1)), Html.attr_literal(\"aria-label\", \"One less\"), Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"rx-49lxy6\"),], [Html.text(\"−\"),]), Html.text(\" \"),\n\t\tHtml.element(\"output\", [Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"rx-49lxy6\"),], [Html.hole_of(|| Html.hole(count!().to_str())),]), Html.text(\" \"),\n\t\tHtml.element(\"button\", [Html.attr_literal(\"type\", \"button\"), Html.on(\"click\", || set_count!(count!() + 1)), Html.attr_literal(\"aria-label\", \"One more\"), Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"rx-49lxy6\"),], [Html.text(\"+\"),]), Html.text(\" \"),\n\t\tHtml.element(\"button\", [Html.attr_literal(\"type\", \"button\"), Html.attr_literal(\"class\", \"reset rx-49lxy6\"), Html.on(\"click\", || set_count!(0)), Html.flag_of(\"disabled\", || count!() == 0),], [\n\t\t\tHtml.text(\"Reset\"),\n\t\t]),\n\t])\n}"}
				first={9}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					What calls something named with <code>!</code>, such as <code>count!()</code>, can change, so
					it becomes a function the browser runs again when the signal changes:
					<code>{"{count!().to_str()}"}</code> is <code>{"Html.hole_of(|| …)"}</code>, and
					<code>{"class:negative={…}"}</code> is <code>{"Html.class_of(\"negative\", || …)"}</code>.
					What reads nothing that changes is written once, as a plain value: an attribute written in the
					file is <code>Html.attr_literal</code>, and text <code>Html.text</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					A handler, <code>{"on:click={…}"}</code>, is a Roc function given to <code>Html.on</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					The props of an effectful component (<code>view!</code>) become getters:
					<code>start_value</code> is now a function, read where the code reads it, so a parent can pass
					a value that changes.
				</li>
				<li>
					The class <code>rx-49lxy6</code> comes from the component's <code>{"<style>"}</code>, which
					rocx scopes to this component's markup and moves into the app's stylesheet.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A route file's Roc goes into the app's <code>Generated/</code> folder instead of beside it. The
				<a href={Paths.syntax}>syntax</a> page has everything you can write in markup,
				<a href={Paths.tooling}>tooling</a> has the commands, and <a href={Paths.safety}>safety</a> shows
				what rocx refuses to build, and why.
			</p>

			<Heading id="pages">rocx's pages</Heading>
			<PageCards pages={Nav.pages_of(Nav.rocx_docs).drop_if(|page| page.path == Paths.rocx)} />
		</NoHydration>
	</>
